)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{3} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}} + \\frac{1}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}.\n$$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186097247 1074 "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x}\\right)=\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n4.\n$$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186097260 1074 "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=8 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 24 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n6 \\pi^{2}.\n$$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186097818 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186097818 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186097917 1064 "We know how to integrate $e^x,$ but how do we integrate a function like $e^{2x}?$\n\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186097917 1064 "We know how to integrate $e^x,$ but how do we integrate a function like $e^{2x}?$\n\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186113628 1078 #edb/ref 1759