139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186380242 1071 "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186380242 1071 "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186380242 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186380242 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186396571 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-y) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=x\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''." 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186396571 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''." 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186396573 1080 "III only" 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186396573 1080 "ÍII only" 13194139542460 17592186576411 false] [17592186404482 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4) &=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation." 13194139542460 17592186576411 true] [17592186404482 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation." 131